TY  - UNPB
AU  - Ban, Byunghyun
TI  - When Post-Publication Modification Is Not Enough - A Case Study of Multi-Layer Scholarly Overlap and Delayed Retraction
PY  - 2026
DO  - 10.13140/RG.2.2.33321.97125
UR  - https://www.researchgate.net/publication/404419191_When_Post-Publication_Modification_Is_Not_Enough_-_A_Case_Study_of_Multi-Layer_Scholarly_Overlap_and_Delayed_Retraction
AB  - Post-publication integrity concerns are often treated as problems of textual similarity, missing citation, or minor correctable error. However, some cases involve deeper forms of scholarly overlap that cannot be remedied through limited post-publication modification. This paper presents a case study of a post-publication complaint involving a 2022 preprint and a subsequent 2023 journal article that was later retracted. The official retraction notice states that an editorial investigation confirmed a high level of overlap between the subsequent article and the prior publication, including similarities in methodology, figures, layout, paper structure, and overall study topic, without appropriate acknowledgement and citation. The notice also states that concerns regarding the origins of the underlying data could not be satisfactorily resolved, leading the Editorial Board to lose confidence in the reliability and originality of the article. Using this case, the paper proposes a multi-layer overlap taxonomy and an editorial remedy framework. The central argument is that post-publication modification may be insufficient when the integrity concern extends beyond wording to methodology, visual materials, manuscript structure, attribution, and data provenance. In such cases, correction or citation addition cannot substitute for a full editorial investigation and, where appropriate, retraction.
LA  - en
M3  - Preprint
